{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello Mr. ___, I’m Dr. ___. You’re here today for your third TACE procedure for the liver tumors. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m doing okay, a bit nervous. The last two went fine, but I just hope this one helps stop the cancer from growing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s completely understandable. The MRI before this showed some remaining active tumor in segments VII and VIII, so we’re targeting those areas again today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will they use the same treatment as last time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll use doxorubicin-loaded beads to deliver chemotherapy directly to the tumors. We also saw a tricky blood vessel from the adrenal area feeding part of the tumor, so we’ll block that with coils to prevent damage to other areas."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Coils? Like metal ones?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Tiny coils, about the size of a small paperclip, to block off that side branch. It’s a safety step so the chemo only goes where we want it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. And the groin — will that hurt after?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2158-12-31 16:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We access the artery through your right groin. You’ll have a small bandage, and we’ll ask you to lie flat for a few hours after to prevent bleeding. Some soreness is normal, but we’ll give you pain medicine if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2158-12-31 19:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. The procedure is done, and it went very well. We treated the main tumor in segment VIII and two smaller ones near the top of your liver."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2158-12-31 19:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t feel much during it. Is that normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2158-12-31 19:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you were given light sedation. Most patients don’t feel pain during the procedure. We used imaging to guide everything precisely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2158-12-31 19:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Did you find the vessel you were worried about?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2158-12-31 19:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did. It came from the adrenal artery and branched into an intercostal vessel feeding the tumor. We placed coils and then used microspheres to block it. That should reduce the risk of the tumor getting blood from that route."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2158-12-31 19:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. Any side effects I should watch for?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2158-12-31 19:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You might have some low-grade fever, mild pain, or nausea over the next few days — that’s called post-embolization syndrome. Take acetaminophen for fever or pain, but no more than 2,000 mg a day. We’ll monitor your liver numbers too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs look stable. No signs of infection or liver failure. We’re planning to discharge you in a couple of hours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. Should I restart all my usual meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue everything — your glyburide, spironolactone, nadolol, and the others. We’re sending prescriptions for ranitidine for 30 days and prochlorperazine in case you feel nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And when do I see the liver doctor again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll follow up with hepatology and oncology in four weeks. They’ll order a new MRI to see how the tumors are responding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if I get a fever at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "A low fever is expected. But if it goes above 101, or you have severe pain, vomiting, or confusion, go to the ER. Those could be signs of infection or liver issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thanks for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2159-01-01 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. You did well today. Rest, stay hydrated, and we’ll see how you’re doing at your follow-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2159-01-01 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just confirming — you’re feeling well, no pain, no nausea, and your groin site looks okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2159-01-01 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, all good. I’m ready to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2159-01-01 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. You’re cleared to go. Take care, and we’ll see you in clinic soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2158-12-31 16:28:00",
    "admission_end": "2159-01-01 11:10:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "Mr. ___, a male with hepatitis C-related cirrhosis and multifocal hepatocellular carcinoma, was admitted for observation following his third transarterial chemoembolization (TACE) procedure targeting residual tumors in liver segments VI, VII, and VIII. The procedure was successfully performed via the right femoral artery, delivering doxorubicin-eluting beads to the tumor sites. A previously suspected phrenic artery supply was not identified, but a feeding vessel from the right adrenal/intercostal artery was successfully coiled and embolized to prevent non-target perfusion. The patient tolerated the procedure well, with no immediate complications, pain, or bleeding at the access site. He remained stable throughout the admission, with mild expected changes in liver enzymes. He was discharged home the following day with instructions to monitor for post-embolization syndrome, continue all home medications, and follow up with oncology and hepatology in four weeks for imaging and treatment reassessment.",
    "problems": [
      "Malignant neoplasm of liver, primary",
      "Cirrhosis of liver without mention of alcohol",
      "Unspecified viral hepatitis C without hepatic coma",
      "Esophageal varices in diseases classified elsewhere, without mention of bleeding",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Old myocardial infarction",
      "Aortocoronary bypass status",
      "Encounter for antineoplastic chemotherapy"
    ]
  }
}
